 uses:'Where is it used?',
 dTitle:'⏳ Watch half-life happen',dHint:'Each square is a radioactive atom. When it decays it goes dark. Notice: after each half-life about half remain, but nobody can tell which atom will decay next!',
 dHl:'Half-life (seconds)',dStart:'▶ Start decay',dReset:'↺ Reset',dRemain:'Atoms remaining',dTime:'Time',dHls:'Half-lives passed',
 cTitle:'⛓️ The uranium-238 decay chain',cSub:'Uranium-238 takes 14 decay steps to become stable lead. Press play to follow the journey.',cPlay:'▶ Play the chain',cEnd:'stable',
 cards:[['🧲','The strong nuclear force','Holds protons and neutrons together. About 100 times stronger than the electric force, but with a very short range.'],['⚖️','Binding energy','The energy needed to pull a nucleus apart. Iron and nickel are the most tightly bound, which is why heavy nuclei release energy when split and light nuclei when fused.'],['🎲','Decay is random','You cannot know when a single atom will decay, but you can predict very precisely how millions of atoms behave together.'],['🧬','Radioactive nuclei in medicine','Isotopes like technetium-99m, iodine-131 and fluorine-18 are used every day to diagnose and treat disease.']]
